    "content": "Somalia's Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation, Abdusalam Abdi Ali, arrived in Djibouti on Sunday to lead the Somali delegation at the 74th extraordinary session of the IGAD Council of Ministers. The meeting, scheduled for September 15, brings together foreign ministers and representatives from IGAD member states.\n\nThe gathering is expected to address the peace and security situation in the region, as well as matters related to the organization's operations. Ministers will review political and security developments across the Horn of Africa, according to the meeting's agenda.\n\nIGAD, the Intergovernmental Authority on Development, is an eight-country trade bloc that includes Somalia, Djibouti, Ethiopia, Kenya, Uganda, Sudan, South Sudan, and Eritrea. The organization has been actively involved in mediating regional conflicts and coordinating responses to humanitarian crises.\n\nThis extraordinary session comes amid ongoing security challenges in the region, including the fight against al-Shabab in Somalia and tensions in Sudan. The meeting provides a platform for member states to align their positions on these issues.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the outcomes of this meeting could have direct implications for security and political stability in Somalia. Diaspora communities often rely on regional cooperation to support family members back home and to facilitate investments and development projects. A unified regional approach to peace and security is seen as crucial for sustainable progress.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/15/soomaaliya-oo-ka-qaybgalaysa-kalfadhiga-degdegga-ah-ee-wasiirrada-igad/",
