    "content": "Somalia's Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation, Absalan Abdi Ali, arrived in Djibouti on Monday to head the Somali delegation at the 74th extraordinary session of the IGAD Council of Ministers. The meeting brings together foreign ministers from IGAD member states to discuss key regional issues and enhance cooperation among Horn of Africa countries.\n\nThe IGAD Council of Ministers is a key decision-making body of the Intergovernmental Authority on Development, an eight-member regional bloc comprising Djibouti, Eritrea, Ethiopia, Kenya, Somalia, South Sudan, Sudan, and Uganda. This extraordinary session was convened to address pressing matters affecting the region, including peace and security, economic integration, and climate resilience.\n\nSomalia's active participation in the summit underscores its commitment to multilateral engagement and its role in shaping regional policies. The minister's attendance follows previous IGAD meetings where Somalia has advocated for collective security and development initiatives.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the outcomes of this summit could have significant implications. IGAD discussions often cover issues such as refugee returns, remittances, and cross-border trade—all of which directly affect diaspora communities. Enhanced regional cooperation may lead to more stable and predictable policies that benefit Somalis abroad.\n\nAs the meeting progresses, further details are expected on specific agreements or initiatives. The Somali delegation is anticipated to push for strengthened partnerships on security and economic development.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/14/wasiirka-arrimaha-dibadda-soomaaliya-oo-gaaray-jabuuti/",
