    "title": "Dried-up water source in Bari mountains leaves thousands in Puntland facing acute shortage",
    "original_title": "Dried up water source in Bari mountains leaves thousands facing acute water crisis",
    "slug": "dried-up-water-source-in-bari-mountains-leaves-thousands-in-puntland-facing-acute-shortage",
    "summary": "A natural water source in Tasjiic, Puntland, dried up in August, leaving over 10,000 people without reliable water access. Tanker water from Waaciye is too expensive for many displaced families and pastoralists.",
    "content": "A natural water source in Tasjiic, a small town in the mountainous Bari region of Puntland, dried up in August, leaving more than 10,000 residents facing a severe water shortage. The affected population includes local families and people displaced by conflict, as well as pastoralists who rely on the source for their livestock. The nearest alternative water supply is in Waaciye, about 40 kilometers away, where tanker deliveries sell for $6 to $8 per 200-liter drum, a price that many cannot afford.\n\nSaytun Mahad Mohamed, a 48-year-old mother of ten, told Radio Ergo that her family depends on 20 liters of water donated by neighbors. When that runs out, she said, they must travel long distances in search of water. The shortage has forced residents to reduce daily consumption, with some families using as little as 2 liters per person per day for drinking, cooking, and hygiene.\n\nThe drying up of the Tasjiic source follows a prolonged drought in Puntland that has already depleted many wells and reservoirs across the region. Local authorities and aid agencies have not yet launched a large-scale relief operation for the affected communities. The lack of water has also hit livestock, the main livelihood for pastoralists in the area, with reports of weakened animals and reduced milk production.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the crisis in Tasjiic is a reminder of the fragility of water access in many parts of the homeland. Diaspora members often send remittances to relatives in Puntland, and some may now face increased requests for emergency support to cover water purchases or transport costs. The situation underscores the need for long-term investments in water infrastructure, including boreholes and storage systems, to help communities withstand recurrent droughts.\n\nWater shortages in Puntland have been exacerbated by climate change, which has made rainfall more erratic and droughts more frequent. In recent years, similar crises have been reported in other parts of Bari and neighboring regions. Without immediate assistance, residents of Tasjiic and surrounding areas could face further displacement as families move toward urban centers like Bosaso in search of water.\n\nHumanitarian organizations operating in Somalia have repeatedly warned that funding gaps are limiting their ability to respond to drought-related needs. The crisis in Tasjiic highlights the importance of supporting local water committees and community-led solutions, which can provide more sustainable access than costly tanker deliveries. As the dry season continues, the situation for the affected families is likely to worsen unless additional aid arrives.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Ergo here: https://radioergo.org/en/2026/09/dried-up-water-source-in-bari-mountains-leaves-thousands-facing-acute-water-crisis/",
    "source": "radioergo.org",
    "source_name": "Radio Ergo",
    "original_url": "https://radioergo.org/en/2026/09/dried-up-water-source-in-bari-mountains-leaves-thousands-facing-acute-water-crisis/",
    "country": "Somalia",
    "category": "Society",
