    "summary": "The Somaliland Human Rights Commission has expressed strong concern over the unlawful detention of poet Ismaaciil Yuusuf (Jiheeye) and journalist Cabdiqani Xuseen Abokor (Baylood), who were arrested without a court warrant and remain in custody without trial.",
    "content": "The Somaliland Human Rights Commission (HRC) has voiced serious concern over the detention of poet Ismaaciil Yuusuf Jiheeye and journalist Cabdiqani Xuseen Abokor Baylood. According to a statement released by the commission, security forces arrested the two men without a court-issued warrant. Their detention has been linked to a recent poem that was recited and subsequently circulated.\n\nThe HRC's statement indicates that both individuals are still being held in prison and have not been brought before any court. The commission described the arrests as unlawful, noting the absence of judicial authorization. This development has raised questions about the respect for legal procedures and freedom of expression in Somaliland.\n\nThe poet Jiheeye and journalist Baylood are well-known figures in their respective fields. Jiheeye is recognized for his literary contributions, while Baylood works as a reporter. Their detention without trial has drawn attention from local and international observers who monitor human rights and press freedom in the region.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this incident is particularly significant. Many diaspora members maintain close ties to their homeland and rely on journalists and artists to stay informed and connected to cultural and political developments. The detention of a poet and a journalist underscores concerns about the shrinking space for free expression, which can affect the flow of information to the diaspora.\n\nThe Somaliland Human Rights Commission continues to call for the release of the two men and for their cases to be handled through proper legal channels. The commission's statement serves as a reminder of the ongoing challenges facing independent voices in the region. The diaspora community, which often advocates for human rights and democratic freedoms, is likely to watch this case closely.\n\nAs of now, no further details have been released about the poem's content or the specific reasons for the arrests. The HRC has urged authorities to either charge the individuals in a court of law or release them immediately. This case highlights the delicate balance between security concerns and fundamental rights in Somaliland.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/14/guddiga-xuquuqul-insaanka-somaliland-oo-ka-walaacay-xarigga-suugaan-yahan-iyo-wariyeyaal/",
