    "title": "Somalia, UNDP Discuss Livestock Sector Development and Alignment with National Plan",
    "original_title": "Soomaaliya iyo UNDP oo Ka Wada-hadlay Horumarinta iyo Kobcinta Wax-soo-saarka Xoolaha",
    "slug": "somalia-undp-discuss-livestock-sector-development-and-alignment-with-national-plan",
    "summary": "Somalia's Minister of Livestock meets UNDP head in Mogadishu to discuss technical cooperation, livestock production, and aligning sector plans with the National Transformation Plan.",
    "content": "On September 14, 2026, Somalia's Minister of Livestock, Forestry and Range, Hassan Hussein Mohamed, held a meeting in Mogadishu with Christophoros Politis, the head of the United Nations Development Programme (UNDP) for Somalia. The discussions centered on strengthening technical cooperation between the two sides, enhancing the production of live livestock, and aligning the livestock sector's plans with the National Transformation Plan (NTP).\n\nThe meeting also reviewed ongoing efforts to support the livelihoods of pastoralist communities. Livestock is a cornerstone of Somalia's economy, contributing significantly to exports and employment. The NTP, Somalia's long-term development framework, prioritizes economic growth and poverty reduction, with the livestock sector identified as a key driver.\n\nPastoralists have long faced challenges such as drought, disease outbreaks, and limited access to markets. The UNDP has been a key partner in providing technical assistance and capacity-building for Somali institutions. This latest engagement signals continued international support for Somalia's efforts to modernize and expand its livestock industry.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, which maintains strong ties to the livestock trade through remittances and business investments, these developments are closely watched. Many diaspora members are involved in livestock export businesses or support pastoralist family members back home. Improved livestock production and market access could translate into greater economic opportunities and stability in rural areas, potentially reducing reliance on humanitarian aid.\n\nThe meeting did not disclose specific new funding or project details, but both sides reaffirmed their commitment to advancing the sector. The alignment with the NTP suggests a coordinated approach to development that integrates livestock into broader national priorities. Further follow-up meetings are expected to outline concrete steps.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/14/soomaaliya-iyo-undp-oo-ka-wada-hadlay-horumarinta-iyo-kobcinta-wax-soo-saarka-xoolaha/",
